a view over the rooftops of Barcelona  parque guell is situated on a hill not  far from the city center it's the  creation of the famously eccentric  architect Antoni gaudí originally  planned as a private preserve for 60  families of Catalonia's elite today it's  a public park and it's just as popular  with Barcelona residents as with  tourists drawing about 8 million  visitors each year  Juwan Roca head at the city's Historical  Museum is an expert on the parks history  it's real exceptional because when gaudi  made this residential area for way he  tried to find the best of nature and the  best of architectural form in the whole  western tradition to make a place which  had a kind of transcendence even a kind  of heaven the guard houses at the  entrance sport flora and fauna motifs  typical of the local incarnation of our  novel called modern yzma catherine the  park was later between 1900 and 1914 it  features a unique harmony of nature and  architecture gaudi plans it all down to  the tiniest detail like the viaducts  integrated into the landscape were there  built in bird's nests  gadi had originally planned a meeting  area for residents between the  colonnades here he had to find the right  scale and to find the right proportion  not only to saying the square which is  about but also to create a written which  goes from classic to anti classic from  culture to nature and if you walk around  you will find the different perspectives  which are not the same all the time so  it's a very dynamic conception of  structure which is rather static Antony  Cody was one of the pioneering  architects of the 20th century in 1900  industrialist ocelli Quayle hired him to  build a residential area overlooking the  city the project failed  and so in 1926 Parc güell was open to  the public  only two of the residential buildings  were actually constructed no buyer was  found so Guardi himself moved into the  model building that was designed by one  of his assistants but he lived in it for  almost 20 years today it houses a museum  devoted to Gerdes life and work among  its exhibits are items of furniture  designed by the architect  the park offers a view towards the  Cathedral of Sagrada família guddi's  most famous work which is still not  completed but the very best view of the  city is from the parks Esplanade  it was originally planned as an open-air  theatre but today from its vantage point  it's the city that's the show the  serpentine-like bench features a special  mosaic technique that Cody favoured  Gaudi decided to use the Train Cadiz  franca this means broken pieces broken  pieces from reused materials to mix the  different elements in a very free way so  that form puts you in the front of the  city and on the other part you can sit  and participate the social life in the  square  local residents have free admission to  the part of the park that's protected as  a national monument to raise funds to  maintain the park an eight hero entrance  fee for tourists was introduced last  year I like the way the stone terraces  and sculptures have been woven into the  landscape they follow the contours of  the land the park is really beautiful  Barcelona's incredible quiet descends an  evening the park güell is not just an  architectural Monument it's a lovely  place to take a break from the city's  hustle and bustle
 

